      "id": "ta5-16",
      "part": "ta",
      "passage": "<p>(1) In many countries, online shopping has grown rapidly over the past decade. Supporters say it saves time and offers more choice. However, the picture is more complex than it first appears.</p><p>(2) The main advantage is convenience. Shoppers can compare prices, read reviews and order products without leaving home. People in remote areas can buy items that are not available in local shops, and busy parents can save hours of travelling and queuing.</p><p>(3) Yet online shopping has hidden costs. Delivery vans make many small trips, which increases traffic and pollution. Packaging waste, especially plastic, is another problem. Moreover, returning an item can be difficult and costly, and some customers receive products that do not match the description.</p><p>(4) Small local shops often suffer most. They cannot always compete with the low prices and huge range of online sellers, and when they close, town centres lose jobs and community spaces. Some customers also miss the advice and personal service that a shop assistant can provide.</p><p>(5) Online shopping is therefore an important tool, not a perfect solution. Its real value will depend on better delivery systems, less packaging and support for local businesses. A balanced approach may be the best way forward.</p>",
